    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d269">

                <pb facs="0277" n="269" sameAs="frus:frus1937v04/pg269"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.1163 Nantungchow/2: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-08-20T16:00:00+09:00">August 20, 1937—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received August 20—9:55 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>285. Reference Gauss’ telegram of August 19, 2 p.m., regarding bombing by
                    Japanese planes of American Mission at Nantungchow. Senior aide to Navy Minister
                    called Assistant Naval Attaché to his <pb facs="0278" n="270" xml:id="pg270"/>
                    office this noon to express regret and to give his assurances that it had not
                    been deliberate, but probably due to bad weather (visibility) and indistinct
                    bombing objectives. He asked for detailed list of places where Americans and
                    American interests were centered in order that Japanese aircraft units might be
                    given orders to take precautions. Such a list as furnished by Consul General
                    Gauss and supplied by us this morning to the Foreign Office was given him.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Shanghai.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
